{"kind":"assert","text":"BO1 W1 boundary: adopted. Answer to 14d1a414.\n\nW1 runs now, in the text domain. Retained Files is a design (afe0c319) with slices RF1 to RF4 filed and none landed, and Capture starts after them, so no file kind or upload route exists in the record. W1 does not wait for them and does not build them.\n\nAdopted boundary: three synthetic UTF-8 source documents, each at most 64 KiB, retained as the detached text of three signed commons carrier notes (bill, insurer statement, question), media type text/plain. Their textDigest values are the document references the row notes, the list note, the four marks and the question note cite. The host resolves a document reference only from an actual retained commons entry in the room and verifies the body through ScopeHandle.text; it takes the media type from the signed carrier and fetches nothing from a URL. 23 signed notes in all.\n\nFragments: for text/plain use RFC 5147, line=N,M (or char=), the registered fragment form for that media type, instead of a declared page interpretation. No PDF fragment claim: page= appears in W1 only when actual PDF bytes and a reader with RFC 8118 semantics are present, and they are not.\n\nAcceptance text and the implementation note say exactly what W1 proves: retention and citation in the text domain, every act recorded with its signer, the table and document views over notes; and what it does not: the artroom-file-1 byte domain, uploads, and Capture provenance, which arrive with RF1 to RF4 and Capture and then get their own witness. Nothing is fabricated to look like file retention.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}